{"id":"u-f5f99da2-edda-44a3-9603-76574d53f8bc","src":"community","title":"Bill Pearl Champion Physique Month 1-4","author":"Brian T","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/rchMid-joe-pearl-champion-physique-month-1-4","desc":"This is the first 16 weeks of Bill Pearl's 20 month to a Champion Physique program, the first few months will serve as a foundational base to build your work capacity, strengthen your connective tissues, and to establish your own proper lifting mechanics through a basic full-body routine, before transitioning later into more advanced, high-volume split. Training is advised to be taken Monday, Wednesday, Friday or Tuesday, Thursday, Saturday.\n\nBill Pearl had always emphasized that is spelt \"train\" and not \"strain\" which means during your sets make sure you have at least 1 or 2 reps left in the tank, if you cannot complete the reps at the rep range without pushing to failure, lower the weight.  Rest in between any set should be from 30 seconds to around 2 minutes.\n\nBill Pearl (1930 – 2022) is a legendary American bodybuilder who four times won the prestigious title of Mr. Universe (in 1953, 1961, 1967, 1971). Bill Pearl remains one of the most talented bodybuilders who competed prior to the creation of Mr. Olympia contest. He was particularly famous due to his huge arms, ideal muscle proportions and longevity as a bodybuilding athlete, capable of victories at such advanced age as 41.\n\nBorn into a poor family and raised performing hard physical work, Bill joined the United States Navy and went to training in Stern’s Gym. There he started his sports career. His main opponents included such outstanding champions as Reg Park, Sergio Oliva, Frank Zane, and later, he was honored with the name “World’s Best-Built Man of the Century.”\n\nAlongside with his competitions, Bill Pearl became a great gym trainer, gym owner, mentor, writer and a good example of vegetarianism.\n\n(I wanted to add that I put sit up but it should be “bent knee sit up bu